## GraphQL Persisted Queries

## The Problem

```
Typical GraphQL request:
POST /graphql
{ "query": "query GetUser($id: ID!) { user(id: $id) { name email avatar orders(first: 10) { id total } } }" }

Problems:
  1. Large payload — query string can be 2-5KB (mobile concern)
  2. Not cacheable — POST requests not cached by CDN
  3. Security — anyone can send arbitrary queries (NoSQL injection risk)
```

## How APQ Works

```
First Request (cache miss):
POST /graphql
{ "extensions": { "persistedQuery": { "version": 1, "sha256Hash": "abc123" } } }
→ 200: { "errors": [{ "message": "PersistedQueryNotFound" }] }

Client retries with full query + hash:
POST /graphql
{ "query": "query GetUser...", "extensions": { "persistedQuery": { "sha256Hash": "abc123" } } }
→ 200: { "data": { "user": {...} } }

Subsequent requests (cache hit):
GET /graphql?extensions={"persistedQuery":{"sha256Hash":"abc123"}}&variables={"id":42}
→ CDN serves cached response for GET requests 🚀
```

## Locked Persisted Queries (Security)

```typescript
// Build-time: extract all queries from client code, register hashes
// src/graphql/queries.graphql → hash → registered in server

// Server: reject any query not in the allowlist
const allowedQueries = new Map([
  ['abc123', 'query GetUser($id: ID!) { user(id: $id) { name email } }'],
  ['def456', 'query ListQuestions { questions { id topic level } }'],
]);

const server = createYoga({
  plugins: [
    usePersistedOperations({
      getPersistedOperation(sha256Hash: string) {
        return allowedQueries.get(sha256Hash) ?? null;
      },
    }),
  ],
});

// Arbitrary queries now rejected:
POST /graphql { "query": "{ __schema { types { name } } }" }
→ 403: Persisted operation only mode
```

## API Observability In Production

## The Three Pillars

```
Metrics  → What is happening? (aggregated numbers)
Logs     → What happened? (individual events)
Traces   → Why did it happen? (causation chain)
```

## Structured Logging

```typescript
// Every request logs a structured object (not a string)
import { logger } from '@/lib/logger'; // Pino or Winston

export async function middleware(req: NextRequest) {
  const start = Date.now();
  const correlationId = req.headers.get('x-correlation-id') ?? crypto.randomUUID();

  const response = await NextResponse.next();

  logger.info({
    type: 'http_request',
    method: req.method,
    path: req.nextUrl.pathname,
    status: response.status,
    duration_ms: Date.now() - start,
    correlation_id: correlationId,
    user_agent: req.headers.get('user-agent'),
  });

  return response;
}
```

## OpenTelemetry Tracing

```typescript
import { trace, context } from '@opentelemetry/api';

const tracer = trace.getTracer('interview-prep-api');

export async function GET(req: Request, { params }: { params: { id: string } }) {
  return tracer.startActiveSpan('question.getById', async (span) => {
    span.setAttributes({ 'question.id': params.id });
    try {
      const question = await db.getQuestion(params.id);
      span.setStatus({ code: SpanStatusCode.OK });
      return Response.json(question);
    } catch (err) {
      span.recordException(err as Error);
      span.setStatus({ code: SpanStatusCode.ERROR });
      throw err;
    } finally {
      span.end();
    }
  });
}
```

## SLO-Based Alerting

```
Traditional alert: error_rate > 1% → page on-call
Problem: single bad minute triggers false positive

SLO-based:
  SLO: 99.9% of requests succeed over 30 days
  Error budget: 0.1% = 43.2 minutes/month

  Alert when burning budget too fast:
  If burning at 14x rate → budget exhausted in 2 days → P1 alert
  If burning at 2x rate → budget exhausted in 15 days → P3 ticket

  Result: fewer false alarms, alert when it actually matters
```

## Key Dashboards

```
Golden Signals (Google SRE):
  1. Latency     — p50, p95, p99 per endpoint
  2. Traffic     — requests per second
  3. Errors      — 4xx rate, 5xx rate
  4. Saturation  — CPU, memory, DB connection pool

Tool: OpenTelemetry → Datadog / Grafana + Tempo / Honeycomb
```
